<p>For much of my life, I've thought that I wanted to be someone else. Or at least be a person <em>like</em> someone else. Sometimes I'd even try to be that person. &quot;That person&quot; has usually been whomever I happened to be reading or watching something about. It's never worked.</p>
<!-- more -->
<p>Instead, maybe I shouldn't work so hard at being whatever kind of person I find interesting this week. Maybe I should be the kind of person I already am. Except, what kind even is that?</p>
<p>Fair question! The short answer is that I like the discovery phase of everything.</p>
<p>I love movies in which something strange is going on and people are trying to figure out what's happening. The problem is that I lose interest once they figure out what's happening and begin solving, killing, or running from whatever it is.</p>
<p>Same with video games. The games I enjoy are the open-world type where I can just walk around and explore and discover things. I don't want to be rushed, or die, or have to start over at some &quot;save point.&quot; I only want to think, &quot;Ooh, what's in here!?&quot; and then see what's in there. If I get stuck somewhere for too long, I lose interest and quit.</p>
<p>Which brings me to my two main hobbies, photography and computers.</p>
<p>Computers provide an endless opportunity for exploration. There are software tools, operating systems, and nearly infinite different ways of using them. I want to try them all. This is why I do things like changing my blogging software every few months or so. I want to know what it's like to use Hugo, WordPress, Ghost, Eleventy, BSSG, Kirby, Pure Blog, and so on. It's fun setting them up, but in order to get the feel for one, I have to go all in and use it for a while. Later, once I feel like, &quot;OK, I get it,&quot;  I move to something else. Some of the time, I realize what I'm doing, but other times I come up with &quot;reasons.&quot;</p>
<p>Todo apps, text editors, note-taking tools, you name it, all have the same effect on me: I want to try them. I want to see how they're configured and what it's like to use them &quot;for real.&quot;</p>
<p>I spent like a year working on a transition to Linux. It was great fun, but once I felt like I'd mostly figured it out and needed to get actual work done, I kind of wanted to just fall back to macOS.</p>
<p>You get the picture.</p>
<p>Speaking of pictures, I want to know what it's like to use a large-format film camera, or a TLR, or an ancient Barnack Leica, or whatever. What's it like to process color film at home? How does scanning negatives with a digital camera work?</p>
<p>Anyway, I'm beginning to understand that this is just the way I am, and I will probably always be this way. I'm never going to be that guy who always shoots film using the same camera. I'm not going to be someone who uses a 10-year-old MacBook but never thinks about trying anything else. And so on.</p>
<p>I'll keep trying things, getting to a point where I &quot;get it&quot; or get bored, and then try something else. I've always thought this was something I needed to fix, but maybe it's just who I am, and I should lean into it.</p>
